In article <3nr5ka$prm@fnnews.fnal.gov>,
Laurelin of Middle Earth <lauri@elwing.fnal.gov> wrote:
>> If you have a current OSF/1 distribution you probably have a VMS-style
>> editor already.  Our EDT+ product is currently being distributed by
>> Digital with Alpha OSF/1 on a CD that is emblazoned with the word
>> "Free!".
>
>Not necessarily.  My AlphaStation 200 was supposed to come with this disk. 
>The disk never arrived -- a serious disappointment.  We're still
>investigating.  Fortunately, after a week or two of total frustration it
>was discovered that a co-worker somewhere else on site had an un-used copy
>of the disk (he's a unix weenie from the start) -- so at least now I have
>a "reasonable" editor.
>
>> EDT+ is an emulation of VAX EDT.  It has a lot of EVE enhancements.
>
>One EVE enhancement that EDT+ does NOT have, and which pains me greatly,
>is that the RETURN key and the ENTER key are not treated equally.  I don't
>suppose that anybody out there has found a cludge to make these two keys
>function identically?  I'm *really* sick of entering
>
>[Do]
>PF1-7		! because the Do key didn't work
>command
><CR>
><DEL><Enter>	! because the <CR> didn't work
>
>(oh, and by the way, is there a way to get the Do key to work?!?!)

I've already responded to the email you sent to us about this but,
since you posted, and since this might be of general interest to EDT
users, the short answer is:  Use "?*" in any string for EDT+ which
prompts for input to make Return work as Enter.  This is a VAX EDT
feature.

To define keys in EDT+, run the edtcode program to find out what the
scan code of your key is (in this case the "Do" key) and to find a key
with a similar definition (in this case PF1-KP7).  Then put a new
definition for the key in question in an EDTINI.EDT file.

In general, if you already have the product and have questions about
EDT+ you can always send mail to bbc@bbc.com.  If you do not have the
product and have general questions -- including where the @#!& is that
CD that was supposed to be shipped with OSF/1, send mail to
sales@bbc.com.

FYI, the Free CD offer will be running out in August since DEC does not
apparently see value in offering this at no charge to their customers.
After August the products will probably require licensing and a normal
charge from Boston Business Computing.
