In article <bland-270495105346@141.214.12.86>, bland@umich.edu says...
>
>We just installed OSF/1 V3.2 (previously had V1.3).  We are now unable 
to
>mount CDROMs that previously had mounted OK.  (DEC's installation CDs 
mount
>OK.  The mount(8) man page says that these CDs are of type "ufs".)  I
>understand from a friend that this issue has been discussed before, but 
he
>didn't remember the details.  Can anyone point me to ANY info on this?  
I
>didn't see anything on this in the FAQ in comp.sys.dec.
>
>Details:
>
>  # mount -r -t cdfs /dev/rz4c /cdrom
>    /dev/rz4c on /cdrom: No such device
>  # mount -r /dev/rz4c /cdrom
>    /dev/rz4c on /cdrom: No valid filesystem exists on this partition
>We have used the 1st form on the command previously and it worked.  That
>is, the CDs we have used previously are of type "cdfs".
>
	I believe that you need only add a '-t cdfs' into your mount
command.  CDROMs come in both UFS and ISO formats; the former is the
default, and is what DEC O/S CD's are.  Most everything else is in
ISO format (e.g. PC CD's).

	Jim Neeland




