My desktop system is a 3000/300LX with 32 MB of memory.  32 MB seems
to be the minimum they're selling these days.  Under 3.2 it performs
nicely with normal X tools, as long as I use the xemacs that DEC
supplies rather than emacs 19.  (I assume this is because xemacs is
based on emacs 18.)  

With CDE, the CDE tools are incredibly slow at starting up.  I find
that as long as I don't use the CDE tools themselves (i.e. I use the
CDE window manager and environment, but run only xterm and other MIT
applications), performance isn't much worse than in a pure MIT
environment.  

This may sound like an odd comment, but some of our users might
actually like that environment.  Even if you continue using xterm and
other MIT tools, doing it in a CDE environment means that you can use
the CDE setup facilities, and that CDE will save and restore your
environment over logoffs.  For users who don't want to learn to edit
.Xdefaults and .xinitrc, this could be a win.  When you go to run one
of the CDE setup tools, it is very slow, but you probably don't change
your setup very often.  

I tried CDE on a 48 MB machine, and it was fine.  Since regular use of
emacs 19 seems to require a 48 MB machine, I'm inclined to think that
we're going to have to upgrade all of our systems to 48 anyway.  I
think this is obscene, but one of our folks is using CDE on a Sun, and
says it is terribly slow on a 64 MB Sparc 20.  Maybe DEC could run a
special on bulk memory purchases for people upgrading to CDE???

Someone suggested making CDE optional.  I recommend using the normal
xdm.  Users who want CDE should symlink .xsession to
/usr/dt/bin/Xsession (I think I've got the name right).  That will put
them into the CDE environment just as if they had used the CDE login.
I have to do this anyway.  We log in using either Kerberos 5 or Engima
Logic login tokens.  I've integrated the software support using SIA.
xdm and login support SIA, but CDE login does not.  So there's no way
for me to login using the CDE login program.




